Represent each nonzero term by (coefficient, exponent, next) in descending order of exponents, e.g., 4x^3+2x+5 ↔ (4,3)→(2,1)→(5,0).
Core merge algorithm for two descending ordered lists:
while (a && b) {
  if (a->exp == b->exp) { int c=a->coef+b->coef; if(c) append(&result,c,a->exp); a=a->next;b=b->next; }
  else if(a->exp > b->exp) { append(&result,a->coef,a->exp); a=a->next; }
  else { append(&result,b->coef,b->exp); b=b->next; }
}
while(a) { append(&result,a->coef,a->exp); a=a->next; }
while(b) { append(&result,b->coef,b->exp); b=b->next; }
Here append allocates a node with fields coef/exp and joins it to the output tail; zero-sum coefficients are skipped. Runtime O(m+n), assuming sorted exponents.

Use a stack to push opening delimiters '(','[','{'. On every closing delimiter, require a nonempty stack whose top matches; pop it; reject mismatches. At end accept only if stack empty.
C algorithm (using char st[1000], int top=-1):
for (int i=0; expr[i]; ++i) {
 char c=expr[i];
 if(c=='('||c=='['||c=='{') st[++top]=c;
 else if(c==')'||c==']'||c=='}') {
   if(top<0) return 0;
   char o=st[top--];
   if(!((o=='('&&c==')')||(o=='['&&c==']')||(o=='{'&&c=='}'))) return 0;
 }
}
return top==-1;
For production code, check stack capacity before push. Time O(n), space O(n) in worst case.
